[ Chuckles ]
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:20.552 --> 00:00:22.722
There she is. You got it.

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.557 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:24.557 --> 00:00:25.891
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:25.891 --> 00:00:27.593
We're here with Ben Stiller.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:27.593 --> 00:00:29.028
That was a clip
from "Severance."

00:00:29.028 --> 00:00:32.999
This is a fantastic show.

00:00:32.999 --> 00:00:34.701
Super specific, super unique.

00:00:34.701 --> 00:00:36.636
And an idea that came to you

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:37.703
years ago.
-Yeah.

00:00:37.703 --> 00:00:39.338
Dan Erikson,
who created the show,

00:00:39.338 --> 00:00:41.173
wrote the script as a sample.

00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:43.276
We got it five years ago,

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:45.211
and I read it
and I thought it was amazing.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:48.714
And it took a few years to
get it going, but we finally --

00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:52.418
We started to produce it
right before the pandemic,

00:00:52.418 --> 00:00:53.753
about six months
before the pandemic.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:54.753
So then we were about to
start shooting,

00:00:54.753 --> 00:00:56.055
then we had to shut down.

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:59.092
So we've been on it
for like two and a half years.

00:00:59.092 --> 00:01:01.527
-Every detail about it
seems perfect,

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:04.264
from the set design, we've been
talking about casting backstage.

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:06.165
Opening credits are amazing.

00:01:06.165 --> 00:01:07.700
Did you --
When you first saw those,

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:08.568
did you just think,

00:01:08.568 --> 00:01:09.869
"Oh, my God"?
-Yeah.

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:13.205
You know, I saw this guy
on Instagram called Extraweg

00:01:13.205 --> 00:01:16.342
that had this -- he had this
weird animation of, like,

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:19.512
babies coming out of a brain
and, like, turning into jelly.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.981
And I thought,
"This is amazing."

00:01:21.981 --> 00:01:23.215
And so I reached out to him,

00:01:23.215 --> 00:01:25.150
and he hadn't done
an opening credits sequence,

00:01:25.150 --> 00:01:27.119
but it felt like his vibe
was right for the show.

00:01:27.119 --> 00:01:29.355
So he worked with us
over the course of time

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:31.023
and like, you know, we're --
getting to know the scripts

00:01:31.023 --> 00:01:32.157
and all that.

00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:34.293
So it's been like a lot of time

00:01:34.293 --> 00:01:36.996
to sort of like mount it in
the way that we wanted to do it.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.364
And it's been fun
to have it come out

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:41.133
because we've been in the bubble
with it for so long.

00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.602
-Yeah.
-And not knowing how people

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.170
would react to it, 'cause it is
kind of weird and specific.

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:47.673
-When you reached out to --
what was his name?

00:01:47.673 --> 00:01:50.043
-Well, he's called Extraweg.
His name is Oliver Latta.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:51.344
He's in Berlin.
-Gotcha.

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:54.480
How long did it take
to convince him you were you?

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:56.249
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:57.583
-Like, I would just think
a guy --

00:01:57.583 --> 00:01:59.285
-Like a catfish sort of thing?
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:01:59.285 --> 00:02:00.553
-No, no, no.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.222
I mean, I set up a Zoom with him
so he could see me.

00:02:03.222 --> 00:02:04.657
-Oh, so he saw your face.
Yeah, that's helpful.

00:02:04.657 --> 00:02:05.891
You can't just reach out
and be like,

00:02:05.891 --> 00:02:08.027
"Hey, this is American director
Ben Stiller."

00:02:08.027 --> 00:02:10.863
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:12.231
We were also --

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.400
You know, "Dannemora,"
which was so fantastic.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.169
And I was watching this
and while I was watching it,

00:02:16.169 --> 00:02:17.270
I was like,
"It's so cool that Ben did

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:18.438
something completely different,"

00:02:18.438 --> 00:02:20.406
but there is really
like a prison element

00:02:20.406 --> 00:02:21.774
to both these shows.
-Yeah.

00:02:21.774 --> 00:02:23.142
-Did you know that
from the jump?

00:02:23.142 --> 00:02:25.678
-No, I'm so dense it took me
about a year into shooting

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:26.846
because I thought,

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:28.514
"Oh, this is really different
than Dannemora."

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:30.449
We're doing this, like,
sci-fi weird thing

00:02:30.449 --> 00:02:32.452
and, you know, it's got
a totally different feel.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:33.653
And then about a year
into shooting,

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:34.921
I'm walking around the hallways
and I said,

00:02:34.921 --> 00:02:36.155
"I feel like I'm in prison."

00:02:36.155 --> 00:02:37.756
And all of a sudden I'm like,
"Wait a minute,

00:02:37.756 --> 00:02:40.793
this is just like
that prison thing I did.

00:02:40.793 --> 00:02:42.262
This is exactly the same.

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:43.796
I haven't done
anything different at all."

00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.965
-[ Laughs ]
-But it is -- it is --

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.033
You know, you realize that

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.535
there's so many
different human elements

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:50.302
that are going on down there,
because they are down there.

00:02:50.302 --> 00:02:52.071
They're not
technically prisoners,

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:53.873
but their life
doesn't exist outside of there.

00:02:53.873 --> 00:02:54.840
-Yeah.
-I mean, they've had their

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.909
consciousness split.

00:02:55.909 --> 00:02:57.309
There's a chip inside their head

00:02:57.309 --> 00:02:59.178
so they don't remember who
they are when they go to work.

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:00.880
They don't remember what
happened when they leave work.

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:03.315
So it's an interesting concept.
-It's a great concept.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:07.720
It's such a fun puzzle, and you
guys put so much care into it.

00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:09.489
And I really enjoy
watching a show like this.

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:11.323
And I feel like,
obviously I'm not alone,

00:03:11.323 --> 00:03:14.059
is it fun having
worked on something so long

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:15.728
to have people respond to it
and all of a sudden be like,

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:16.996
"Oh, we made
a thing people love"?

00:03:16.996 --> 00:03:19.899
-Yeah, it's great. I mean,
it's such a nice feeling.

00:03:19.899 --> 00:03:21.734
But there's also -- I've never
really been a part of a show

00:03:21.734 --> 00:03:23.469
that has
that sort of interest in it,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:24.503
where people are wondering

00:03:24.503 --> 00:03:25.705
what's gonna happen
the next week

00:03:25.705 --> 00:03:27.272
and, you know,
kind of a "Lost" type that --

00:03:27.272 --> 00:03:28.674
I never watched "Lost," really.

00:03:28.674 --> 00:03:30.576
But I know, you know, I know
what that kind of thing is.

00:03:30.576 --> 00:03:32.478
So it's been fun to see

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:34.847
how people speculate
and have these theories.

00:03:34.847 --> 00:03:36.849
But also the reality
is we finished it.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.285
It's done, so people will
be like, "I hope he doesn't,"

00:03:39.285 --> 00:03:40.619
you know, "I hope she's alive.

00:03:40.619 --> 00:03:42.622
I hope this doesn't turn out
to be that they're real."

00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:44.256
And I'm like,
"It already happened, I don't --

00:03:44.256 --> 00:03:46.092
I really hope you like it,

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:47.694
because there's
nothing I can do about

00:03:47.694 --> 00:03:49.462
what's coming on next week."
-Right.

00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:51.030
It's not like "SNL,"
where it's like,

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:52.198
"I hope next week they do this."

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:53.199
Oh, it's too late.

00:03:53.199 --> 00:03:54.767
-It's just
it's already baked in.

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:57.703
-And again, it must be so much
fun to do a show that's a hit.

00:03:57.703 --> 00:04:00.806
We were talking backstage about
"The Ben Stiller Show" on Fox,

00:04:00.806 --> 00:04:02.374
which won an Emmy.
-Yes.

00:04:02.374 --> 00:04:04.376
-And --
-Not a hit, though.

00:04:04.376 --> 00:04:05.778
-Not a hit.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:07.346
Well, I think we can speak to it

00:04:07.346 --> 00:04:08.480
because --
-It's so funny, I went to see

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.283
Billy Joel the other night,
who was amazing.

00:04:10.283 --> 00:04:12.017
And before every song that
we've all heard a million times,

00:04:12.017 --> 00:04:13.453
he would say,
"This was not a hit,

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.820
this was not a hit, this was..."

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.322
because at the time,
they weren't.

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:17.690
But "The Ben Stiller Show"
was canceled

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.158
after, you know,
like 12 episodes.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:21.193
-It won the Emmy
after it was canceled, right?

00:04:21.193 --> 00:04:22.962
-Yeah, we'd been canceled
for nine months.

00:04:22.962 --> 00:04:24.564
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.564 --> 00:04:26.732
-I think that speaks
to how a show endures,

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:29.034
that people got it so wrong.
-Yeah, no.

00:04:29.034 --> 00:04:31.503
I mean, we were all shocked
and very happy, but it was.

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:33.272
-Did you walk up
and go "It wasn't a hit"?

00:04:33.272 --> 00:04:35.174
[ Laughs ]
-I did something like that.

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:38.277
I did some stupid
young person's speech.

00:04:38.277 --> 00:04:41.346
-Well, for anybody who --
If you finish "Severance,"

00:04:41.346 --> 00:04:43.082
I think go back and watch
"The Ben Stiller Show,"

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.184
a very nice companion piece.
-Oh, perfect.

00:04:45.184 --> 00:04:46.352
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:48.187
-Great to see you, man.
-Ben Stiller.

00:04:48.187 --> 00:04:49.721
A new episode of "Severance"
is available

00:04:49.721 --> 00:04:51.257
this Friday on Apple TV+.

